After Java 8, Java added several important improvements.

Java 9 — Module system (JPMS):
The Java Platform Module System (JPMS) introduces modules (module-info.java) for strong encapsulation and explicit dependencies, improving modularity and allowing custom runtime images.

Java 10 — Local-variable type inference (var):
Local variables can use `var` and let the compiler infer the type, reducing verbosity for local declarations.

Java 11 — LTS and standard HTTP client:
Java 11 is an LTS release and standardized the java.net.http.HttpClient (supporting HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2, synchronous and asynchronous usage). It also added convenient String and Files APIs (e.g., String.isBlank(), String.lines(), Files.readString(), Files.writeString()).

Later releases — Switch expressions, text blocks, records, and pattern matching:
Newer Java releases introduced switch expressions (switch as an expression with -> labels), text blocks (multiline string literals), records (concise immutable data carriers), and pattern matching for cleaner type tests and extraction.

Java 17 — LTS and sealed classes:
Java 17 is another LTS release and finalized sealed classes, which restrict which classes or interfaces may extend or implement a sealed type.

Java 21 — Virtual threads and sequenced collections:
Java 21 introduced virtual threads (lightweight threads for massive concurrency, Project Loom) and sequenced collection interfaces (unified APIs for collections with a defined sequence, e.g., head/tail operations).
